      "text": "The cost is a worst-case information budget, with failure probability assigned to correctness. Bounding only the expected exploration size would be a different question. Likewise, success at one requested vertex is weaker than saying that all answers are simultaneously correct. These distinctions are significant when small failure probabilities are amplified across an entire graph."
    },
    {
      "text": "There is already a strong speedup theorem: below √log n, randomized local computation can be replaced by deterministic O(log* n) computation. The conjectured threshold is log n. A candidate at, for example, (log n)^(3/4) would escape the existing theorem while remaining strictly below the proposed threshold.",
      "citation": "gap"
    },
    {
      "text": "Logarithmic randomized volume really occurs. The Lovász local lemma and sinkless orientation provide benchmark behavior at that scale in the cited work. The question is whether the whole interval immediately below log n is empty, beyond the familiar tiny-information symmetry-breaking regime.",
      "citation": "gap"
    },
    {
      "text": "Peng’s July 2026 constructions provide Θ((log n)^k) for positive integers k and polynomial scales up to polylogarithmic factors. Since the smallest exponent k is one, these do not insert a problem into the sublogarithmic interval. Figure 2 of that paper continues to mark the interval from √log n to log n as unknown.",
      "citation": "recent"
    }
