  "status": "resolved",
  "status_note": "Resolved affirmatively by Ghaffari, FOCS 2022. The inherited WOLA 2019 page remains an outdated open-question source. The current card preserves that history and states the theorem answering its polynomial-probe existence target.",
  "formulation_reviewed_on": "2026-09-10",
  "key": "local-computation-mis-polynomial-degree-resolved",
  "context_blocks": [
    {
      "text": "A maximal independent set can contain a linear number of vertices. In a large implicit graph, a user may need only the membership of one vertex and have no reason to generate the entire set. An LCA supplies that membership on demand. Different answers must nevertheless describe one valid global solution: independently choosing a convenient set for each request could select both endpoints of an edge."
    },
    {
      "text": "The target is maximality under inclusion, not maximum cardinality. A globally run greedy algorithm already computes a maximal independent set efficiently. The local difficulty is reconstructing the requested decision without following a chain of dependencies through a large part of the graph. Reusing fixed randomness allows separate requests to refer to the same implicit computation."
    },
    {
      "text": "The degree dependence is central. A bound with an exponent growing as log log Δ is much larger than a fixed polynomial when Δ grows. The 2019 workshop question asks to remove that growing exponent while keeping dependence on n polylogarithmic. Its archived wording still presents this as an open question.",
      "citation": "problem"
    },
    {
      "text": "The 2022 algorithm resolves that target by carefully limiting which parts of a randomized distributed computation must be explored. Directly expanding a whole neighborhood for every simulated round is too expensive; controlling the actual dependencies is what permits a fixed polynomial in Δ. The remaining sparse components can then be completed consistently.",
      "citation": "solution"
    },
    {
      "text": "This resolved card stays in the catalogue to correct the inherited status and preserve its history. It also provides a useful reference point for more demanding targets, such as a linear or quadratic dependence on Δ. Those strengthen the question and should receive their own precise formulation and source if included."
    }
