    {
      "text": "The known landscape has substantial empty intervals. A gap theorem can tell us that no problem occupies an interval without giving an effective way to determine which side a supplied rulebook occupies. The regular-tree classification paper explicitly marks the gap between randomized doubly logarithmic and logarithmic complexity as having unknown decidability.",
