  "status_note": "The April 2025 primary paper explicitly leaves arbitrary drawings unresolved while proving the spherical-arc case. No general proof or counterexample located through 10 September 2026. Restricted drawing-class results are not treated as full resolutions.",
  "formulation_reviewed_on": "2026-09-10",
  "key": "hill-complete-graph-crossing-number",
  "context_blocks": [
    {
      "text": "A complete graph asks every pair of vertices to communicate through a separate drawn edge. Planarity fails already at five vertices. The conjecture proposes an exact measure of the unavoidable interference as the number of vertices grows, independent of the choice of drawing coordinates or the shape of each edge."
    },
    {
      "text": "The upper bound is constructive: there are systematic drawings with H(n) crossings. The challenge is universal. A lower bound must rule out every different way to route the edges, including configurations whose geometry bears little resemblance to the known constructions. Counting crossings in a visually natural drawing establishes only the upper bound."
    },
    {
      "text": "The topological and geometric versions must be separated. Straight-line edges impose extra restrictions; shortest arcs on a sphere allow more freedom but still impose a geometric rule. Two-page drawings impose another rule: vertices lie on one spine and each edge stays on one of its two sides. Proving the formula for any of these families narrows the search for a counterexample without exhausting arbitrary curves.",
      "citation": "pages"
    },
    {
      "text": "General lower bounds are already close to the conjectured leading term. The flag-algebra approach derives inequalities from the frequencies and compatibility of small drawing configurations. Even a remaining gap of roughly one or two percent matters here: the goal is an exact extremal formula for all sizes, rather than a constant-factor estimate.",
      "citation": "bounds"
    },
    {
      "text": "The April 2025 spherical-arc theorem is a substantial geometric advance: it establishes the conjectured lower bound for every shortest-geodesic drawing on the sphere. Its statement does not say that an optimal unrestricted drawing must be of this type. That missing reduction is why the original question remains open.",
      "citation": "spherical"
    }
