{
  "id": "TCS-5411",
  "title": "Succinct dynamic rank/select in a polynomial universe — resolved",
  "area": "Data structures and compressed data",
  "criterion": "resources",
  "question_type": "yes_no",
  "formal": "Can a dynamic set S⊆{0,…,U−1}, with n=|S| and polynomially larger universe U=n^(1+Θ(1)), be represented using H(n,U)+o(H(n,U)) bits while supporting insertion, deletion, rank and select in deterministic O(1+log n/log log(n+2)) worst-case time per operation on a word RAM with multiplication and word size Θ(log U)? Here H(n,U)=log₂ binom(U,n). Yes: Domingues’s 2026 construction implies this bound.",
  "definitions": "Fix any constants δ>0 and K≥1+δ. The bounds must hold uniformly whenever n≥2 and n^(1+δ)≤U≤n^K, with constants allowed to depend on δ,K. U is fixed during an operation sequence and n may change by one per update; both endpoints of each update are in the stated regime. Insert(x) adds an absent key, Delete(x) removes a present key, Rank(x)=|{y∈S:y≤x}| for any x∈{0,…,U−1}, and Select(j) returns the j-th smallest key for 1≤j≤n. Invalid select arguments return a failure symbol. These are set updates, not insertions that shift the positions of a bit string. The machine has w=⌈log₂(U+1)⌉-bit words and unit-cost reads/writes, comparisons, addition/subtraction, bitwise Boolean operations, shifts and word multiplication. All operations are exact, deterministic and terminating. Initialization from the initial set may take polynomial time in U. Space uses the M_B convention: unused gaps below the highest allocated address count, including partially used final words. Count all stored metadata and lookup tables; a fixed table of polylog(w) words computable from w is allowed and fits in the asymptotic redundancy here. No free input-dependent advice is provided. During an update use the larger of the pre- and post-update space budgets. Succinctness means that the redundancy divided by H(n,U) tends to zero uniformly over the fixed polynomial regime.",
  "answer_criterion": "The historical existence target is resolved affirmatively with deterministic worst-case operations. This does not assert o(n) redundancy at that same deterministic worst-case time, nor a bound for every universe density. The precise target is vanishing redundancy relative to H(n,U).",
  "context": "Rank translates a key into its position in sorted order, while select translates a position back into a key. Together they support predecessor search and form an interface used inside compressed representations. A fully indexable dictionary must answer rank even when the queried key is absent, which is stronger than an index that only accepts stored keys.\n\nThere are binom(U,n) possible sets of a fixed cardinality, so H(n,U) bits is the basic information requirement. A conventional search tree also stores navigation information and counts, whose overhead can be substantial. Succinctness asks for a representation whose extra bits become a negligible fraction of that information requirement.\n\nThe word “succinct” needs its formula. In the polynomial-universe regime here, H is Θ(n log n). An overhead of n times a fractional power of log n is therefore succinct even though it exceeds n bits. The 2025 source uses relative redundancy as its definition; silently replacing that target by o(n) extra bits would create a stronger question.\n\nStatic indexing can inspect the entire set once and organize an elaborate compressed representation. Updates must preserve both the indexing information and the encoding as the set changes. The 2025 static FID result explicitly left the dynamic time-and-space combination as a separate question.\n\nTwo subsequent results address different guarantees. The SODA 2026 construction gets much smaller redundancy with amortized expected operation time. Domingues’s construction gives deterministic worst-case time with a larger but still negligible relative overhead. The latter directly answers the stronger time convention fixed in this card.",
  "why": "Rank/select is a basic ordered-data interface. Achieving its optimal dynamic time together with near-minimum encoding space shows that compression need not cost an additional asymptotic slowdown, and distinguishes space spent on information from space spent on navigation.",
  "importance": {
    "score": 84,
    "method": "editorial",
    "assessed_on": "2026-09-10",
    "reason": "A basic time-space frontier for a widely used ordered-data primitive. The historical existence question is now resolved; its importance is preserved while resolved cards sort below open candidates."
  },
  "progress": [
    {
      "date": "2025-06",
      "text": "Liang and Zhou give optimal static FID trade-offs and explicitly leave succinct dynamic FIDs with O(log n/log log n) operation time open for polynomial universes. Their definition of succinctness is redundancy o(H), not necessarily o(n).",
      "citation": "original"
    },
    {
      "date": "2025-10 / SODA 2026",
      "text": "Kuszmaul, Liang, and Zhou give dynamic insert/delete/rank/select with optimal amortized expected time and redundancy n/2^((log n)^Ω(1))+polylog U in the polynomial regime. This addresses the earlier existence question with randomized amortized guarantees.",
      "citation": "soda"
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-03",
      "text": "Domingues’s Theorem 1 gives deterministic worst-case O(1/ε+log_w n) operations with H+O(n w^ε/ε) bits and a polylog(w)-word table. The construction also supports selecting zero bits in the complement.",
      "citation": "resolution"
    },
    {
      "date": "2026 — implication for this card",
      "text": "Set ε=1/4 and w=Θ(log U)=Θ(log n). The redundancy is O(n(log n)^(1/4))+polylog(log n), which is o(n log n)=o(H), and log_w n=Θ(log n/log log n). Thus the theorem supplies exactly the required deterministic worst-case succinct structure.",
      "citation": "resolution"
    }
